\c 4
\s1 Boaz Redeems Ruth
\p \v 1 \w Meanwhile, Boaz|strong="H1162"\w* \w went|strong="H5927"\w* \w to the gate|strong="H8179"\w* \w and sat down|strong="H3427"\w* \w there|strong="H8033"\w*. \w Soon|strong="H2009"\w* \w the kinsman-redeemer|strong="H1350"\w*\f + \fr 4:1 \ft The Hebrew word for \fqa kinsman-redeemer \ft or \fqa guardian-redeemer\ft is a legal term for the kinsman who redeems or vindicates a relative; also in verses 3, 6, 8, and 14; see \ref Leviticus 25:25–55|LEV 25:25-55\ref*.\f* \w of whom he|strong="H1162"\w* \w had spoken|strong="H1696"\w* \w came along|strong="H5674"\w*, \w and Boaz said|strong="H559"\w*, “\w Come over|strong="H5493"\w* \w here|strong="H6311"\w*, \w my friend|strong="H6423"\w*, \w and sit down|strong="H3427"\w*.” \w So he went over|strong="H5493"\w* \w and sat down|strong="H3427"\w*.
